<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/expert/computational-linguistics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Computational linguistics (NLP) is <strong>language processing by machine</strong> — from the
    rule-based pioneers through statistical revolution to today's transformer models. It is
    simultaneously applied linguistics and AI's hardest text problem, and it feeds discoveries
    back into linguistic theory.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="rule-based, statistical, embeddings, transformers" widget="equationStep" config={compolingCfg} />

  <h2>The tasks and their fates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Machine translation:</strong> rule-based (1960s–80s, ALPAC report killed funding 1966) → statistical (IBM, Google 2006) → neural transformers (2016→). The quality jump at each stage reshaped what "understanding" the field thought necessary.</li>
    <li><strong>Speech recognition and synthesis:</strong> from HMMs to neural end-to-end — voice assistants, transcription, and now real-time dubbing. Phonetics (the first lesson) runs the speech stack.</li>
    <li><strong>Information extraction and search:</strong> named entities, relation extraction, semantic search — the plumbing of the modern internet (every search engine is an NLP system).</li>
    <li><strong>What linguists do now:</strong> evaluate and probe LLMs (do they know syntax, pragmatics, world knowledge?), document low-resource languages before data runs out, and build the evaluation standards (the evaluation lesson's toolkit). The subfield is thriving — with new questions about what these models actually know.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brown et al., "A Statistical Approach to Machine Translation" (1990); Vaswani et al. (2017); Jurafsky & Martin, Speech and Language Processing (3rd ed., free).</p>
</TopicPage>
